---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const gainCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'source', label: 'source level', min: 20, max: 100, step: 1, value: 60, unit: 'dB' },
    { name: 'preamp', label: 'preamp gain', min: 0, max: 60, step: 1, value: 30, unit: 'dB' },
    { name: 'noise', label: 'system noise floor', min: 5, max: 40, step: 1, value: 15, unit: 'dB' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'signal level', expr: 'source + preamp', unit: 'dB' },
    { label: 'signal-to-noise ratio', expr: 'source + preamp - noise', unit: 'dB' },
    { label: 'headroom to clip (0 dBFS≈100)', expr: '100 - (source+preamp)', unit: 'dB' },
  ],
};

const signalCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · The source', note: 'Voice, strings, drum — an acoustic event with its own spectrum and dynamics. Nothing downstream can invent what the room and player never made.' },
    { math: '2 · Microphone: pressure to voltage', note: 'Diaphragms convert sound to electricity. Dynamic mics are rugged and forgiving; condensers are detailed and demand power (phantom +48V).' },
    { math: '3 · Placement is the first EQ', note: 'Distance changes tone and room blend; off-axis changes brightness. Moving the mic beats correcting it later.' },
    { math: '4 · Gain staging: levels down the chain', note: 'Every stage has a noise floor and a clipping ceiling. Set each stage\'s gain so signal stays far above noise and far below clip.' },
    { math: '5 · Capture, don\'t rescue', note: 'Digital editing can fix timing and pitch, but a badly recorded source is a permanent tax. The recording IS the production.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/music-technology/foundational/recording-fundamentals">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Recording is the art of converting a performance into a signal — cleanly, at the right level,
    with choices made deliberately. Its foundation is the signal chain (source → microphone →
    preamp → converter) and the discipline of gain staging: setting each stage's level so the
    music stays far above the noise floor and far below distortion.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the signal chain and its two enemies" widget="equationStep" config={signalCfg} />

  <h2>Gain staging, computed</h2>
  <p>
    Two failures bookend every recording: noise (signal too close to the floor) and clipping
    (signal into the ceiling). The slider bank models both — raise the preamp and watch
    signal-to-noise improve while headroom shrinks. Every professional recording is this
    negotiation, staged correctly at every link so the mix engineer never fights the raw takes.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="signal, noise and headroom down the chain" widget="sliderLab" config={gainCfg} />

  <h2>Microphone choice and placement</h2>
  <p>
    Dynamic mics (SM57 family) handle loud sources and rough stages; large-diaphragm condensers
    catch vocal detail but demand quiet rooms and +48V phantom power. Placement is the cheaper
    and mightier tool: distance blends in room reflections, axis changes tone, and the acoustic
    guitar's sweet spot moves with every millimetre. Experienced engineers change the mic's
    position before reaching for EQ.
  </p>

  <h2>The room is a band member</h2>
  <p>
    Every recording includes the space it happened in — reflections arrive milliseconds after the
    direct sound and define its character. Bedrooms are famously boxy (small-room comb filtering);
    treated rooms and close-miking tame them. You can add artificial room later, but removing a
    bad room is one of audio's impossible tasks — which is why the discipline teaches listening
    to the space before pressing record.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Edexcel A Level Music Technology specification; Huber &amp; Runstein, <em>Modern Recording Techniques</em>; Sound on Sound recording guides.</p>
</TopicPage>
